 ## Overview
-Windowing is a scalar-to-color mapping technique that restricts the visualization to a specific range of data values, mapping those values to a linear grayscale ramp while clamping out-of-range values to black or white [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of low contrast in volumetric data by isolating a "window" of interest (e.g., soft tissue) from the full data range, preventing background noise or irrelevant high-intensity structures (e.g., bone) from obscuring the target features [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Colormap Application for Visualization](7714-colormap-application-for-visualization.md), which maps the entire domain to a spectrum, or [Clamping](11530-clamping.md), which maps out-of-range values to the boundary colors of the transfer function, windowing explicitly discards out-of-range information into neutral background colors [S1, unofficial][S2].
+Windowing is a scalar-to-color mapping technique that restricts the visualization to a specific range of data values, mapping those values to a linear grayscale ramp while clamping out-of-range values to black or white [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of low contrast in volumetric data by isolating a "window" of interest (e.g., soft tissue) from the full data range, preventing background noise or irrelevant high-intensity structures (e.g., bone) from obscuring the target features [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Colormap Application for Visualization](7714-colormap-application-for-visualization.md), which maps the entire domain to a spectrum, or [Clamping](11530-clamping.md), which maps out-of-range values to the boundary colors of the transfer function, windowing explicitly discards out-of-range information into neutral background colors [S1, unofficial].
 
